ogles2 SDL archive: SDL2 and SDL3 for AmigaOS 3 with OpenGL ES 2.0
=====================================================================

SDL2 (2.33.0) and SDL3 (3.4.14) for 68k AmigaOS 3, built from Steffen
Haeuser's SDL2_68k and SDL3_68k with the ogles2 project's OpenGL ES 2.0
backend: SDL programs draw through the PiStorm's GPU (ogles2.library).

  - SDL's 2D API (SDL_Renderer, textures, rotation, blending) is drawn by
    the GPU through SDL's GLES2 renderer ("opengles2"), with no GL code in
    the program.
  - A program that asks SDL for an OpenGL ES 2.0 context gets one from
    ogles2.library: its own shaders, framebuffer objects, vsync
    (SDL_GL_SetSwapInterval), windows resized without losing the context.
  - Any other GL request goes to minigl.library, as in the SDL_68k
    releases; audio, input, timers and threads are SDL_68k's.

This is for programmers. A finished SDL program carries SDL inside it (these
are static link libraries); its users need only ogles2.library and the
shader compiler (the ogles2 archive's Install-ogles2), and minigl.library
29.1 only if the program uses desktop OpenGL.

Contents (unpack over the ogles2 SDK archive: the drawers merge)
--------

  SDK/SDL2/include/SDL2/   SDL2's headers
  SDK/SDL2/lib/            libSDL2.a, libSDL2_test.a
  SDK/SDL3/include/SDL3/   SDL3's headers
  SDK/SDL3/lib/            libSDL3.a, libSDL3_test.a
  SDK/MiniGL/              PiStorm3D's minigl.library 29.1 client SDK
                           (headers, libminigl.a): SDL links it for its
                           desktop-GL path, and SDL_opengl.h includes it
  SDK/doc/SDL.md           how to use them, flags, examples, the figures
  SDK/examples-sdl/        sdl2_sprites.c (2D renderer), sdl3_gles2.c (own
                           GLES2 code), Makefile
  Examples/SDL/            the two examples, built
  licences/                SDL's licence (zlib), MiniGL's

Building a program (bebbo's m68k-amigaos-gcc 6.5, clib2)
--------

  -O2 -m68040 -mhard-float -mcrt=clib2 -fbbb=abcfhilmnprsz0 -D__AMIGAOS3__
  SDL3 also -fshort-wchar

  SDL2: -lSDL2 -logles2 -lminigl -ldebug -lamiga -lm881 -lstubs
  SDL3: -lSDL3 -logles2 -lminigl -ldebug -lamiga -lm881 -latomic -lstubs

Include paths: SDK/include (ogles2), SDK/SDL2/include or SDK/SDL3/include,
SDK/MiniGL/minigl-shared-library/include. Link -lm881, not -lm (with clib2,
-lm is libnix's maths). The libraries do not link with libnix or vbcc
programs. SDK/examples-sdl/Makefile shows it all.

SDL opens ogles2.library itself (when it loads the GL library); do not call
OGLES2Open() in an SDL program.

Shaders
--------

SDL's GLES2 renderer, and most GLES2 programs, write shaders at run time:
the first run compiles each one with the shader compiler (LIBS:v3dglslc,
about a second each) and keeps the result in OGLES2Cache: (or T:); later
runs load it. To ship a program without the compiler, run it once and ship
the .v3dp and .v3ds files from the cache in a shaders/ drawer beside it
(SDK/doc/GettingStarted.txt, section on shaders).

Requirements
--------

A PiStorm with a Raspberry Pi 4B or CM4, Emu68, AmigaOS 3.x, an RTG system
(Picasso96 or CyberGraphX) and a 32-bit RTG screen; ogles2.library 1.3 and
its shader compiler.

